17. WTO and International Trade
Paper: International Economics (ECNCC301) | Unit: III | Topic: WTO and international trade
Introduction
The World Trade Organization (WTO), established on 1 January 1995 after the Uruguay Round, replaced the GATT (1947) as the institution governing multilateral trade rules. It has its headquarters in Geneva and has 160-plus members, including India as a founding member.
Objectives
• Reduce tariffs and other trade barriers.
• Ensure non-discriminatory, transparent and predictable trade.
• Settle disputes through a binding mechanism.
• Promote sustainable development and help developing countries integrate.
Principles
1. Most-favoured-nation (MFN): equal treatment of all members.
2. National treatment: imports treated no less favourably than domestic goods once they enter.
3. Reciprocity and binding of tariffs.
4. Transparency: notifications and trade policy reviews.
5. Special and differential treatment for developing and least-developed countries.
6. Prohibition of quantitative restrictions and tariffication.
Agreements
GATT 1994 (goods), GATS (services), TRIPS (intellectual property), TRIMs (investment measures), Agreement on Agriculture, SPS and TBT, Anti-Dumping, Subsidies and Countervailing Measures, Safeguards, and Textiles (phased out in 2005).
Structure and dispute settlement
Ministerial Conference (highest body, meets every two years), General Council, specialised councils and committees, and the Secretariat. The Dispute Settlement Body uses consultations, panels, the Appellate Body and authorised retaliation. The Appellate Body has been non-functional since 2019 due to the blocking of appointments.
Achievements
Lowered average tariffs, expanded rules to services and IP, and offered a rules-based forum to small countries.
Criticisms and challenges
• The Doha Development Round (2001) stalled over agriculture subsidies and developing-country concerns.
• Rich countries retain agricultural subsidies; developing countries face high non-tariff barriers.
• Decision-making by consensus is slow; regional agreements proliferate.
• India has raised issues on public stockholding for food security and TRIPS and has been a prominent voice for developing countries.
Conclusion
The WTO remains central to global trade governance but faces reform pressure on dispute settlement, subsidies and development issues.